.he "ioctl (2)                    10/28/26                     ioctl (2)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ioctl
- control a device
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
C
.in +5
.br
.bd
int ioctl(int fd, int cmd, char *arg);
.sp 2
.bd
ioctl(fd, cmd, arg)
.br
.bd
int fd, cmd;
.br
.bd
char *arg;
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Ioctl
runs the request
.bd
cmd
against the driver of the device named by
.bd
fd.
A device is a block device or a character device, and the
request means a different thing on each.  A device that
does not have the request answers ENOTTY; there is no
default request.
.sp
.bd
BLOCK DEVICES
.sp
A block device has one request,
.bd
CDBCMD,
which runs the command block
.bd
arg
describes.  The command block is the bus's own - a SCSI
command descriptor block, the seven task-file registers
of an ATA command, or the HD-DMA board's twelve-byte
block - and the kernel copies it, checks its length and
moves its data, but never interprets it.  A block driver
that has no command-block interface has no ioctl and
answers ENOTTY.
.sp
On
.bd
CDBCMD
.bd
arg
points at this structure.  UINT8 is an unsigned byte,
INT16 a signed sixteen-bit integer and UINT32 an
unsigned thirty-two-bit integer, as in the header.
.nf
.bd 9
struct cdb {
    UINT8 cmd[26];      /* the command block itself */
    INT16 len;          /* how many bytes of cmd[] are the command */
    INT16 count;        /* data phase in bytes; 0 if there is none */
    INT16 flags;        /* CDB_IN, CDB_ASYNC; 0 for neither */
    UINT8 *buf;         /* the caller's buffer for the data phase */
    INT16 hole;         /* offset in cmd[] of the data address, or -1 */
    UINT32 addrbits;    /* bit n marks cmd[n..n+2] as an address */
};
.fi
.sp
The requests and the two limits are:
.nf
.bd 5
CDBCMD      0       run the command block in cmd[]
CDB_IN      1       flags: the device fills buf, rather than reading it
CDB_ASYNC   2       flags: do not wait for the answer (network card)
CDBMAX      26      the largest len, the width of cmd[]
CDBDATA     4096    the largest data phase, one page
.fi
.sp
.bd
cmd
holds up to
.bd
CDBMAX
bytes.  The first byte is the bus's opcode where the bus
has one, and what the remaining bytes mean is the
command's own business; the driver is the only part of
the system that knows which bus it is talking to.
.bd
len
says how many of the bytes in
.bd
cmd
are the command (the rest are status space or unused).
.bd
count
is the number of bytes in the data phase, or zero if
the command has none.
.bd
flags
is zero, plus
.bd
CDB_IN
if the device fills
.bd
buf
rather than reading it, plus
.bd
CDB_ASYNC
if the call is not to wait for the answer - the network
card's frames then arrive on its frame stream instead.
.sp
.bd
buf
is the caller's buffer for the data phase; it is
ignored when
.bd
count
is zero.  A data phase of one disk block (512 bytes) or
less is staged in one cache block by the kernel.  A
larger one, up to
.bd
CDBDATA
(one page), is moved by the driver itself, because a
cache block is all the kernel can hold at once.
.sp
A bus that is a bus master needs the data's physical
address, not the virtual address
.bd
buf
names.  The kernel keeps the staged data in a 4096-byte
segment, so its physical address is
.sp
.nf
	(segment << 12) | (window address & 0xfff)
.fi
.sp
and
.bd
hole
says where in
.bd
cmd
that address belongs: at cmd[hole], cmd[hole+1] and
cmd[hole+2], little endian, low byte first.  It is -1
when the command has no data phase, or when the bus
keeps the data address itself (the SCSI adapter does),
so the driver has nothing to write.
.sp
.bd
addrbits
is needed when a command names memory of its own
besides the data phase - a format lays down a
sector-header table, or a list of sectors to skip.
Which words of
.bd
cmd
those addresses occupy is the command's business, not
the driver's, so the caller marks them.  Bit n of
.bd
addrbits
set means cmd[n], cmd[n+1] and cmd[n+2] are one
address; the driver writes the swizzled address shown
above at every marked word.  A word at n with n+2 past
.bd
len
is ignored, and an address that is meant to be zero -
a null sector table - leaves its bit clear.
.bd
hole
is the same thing for the one data-phase address and
the two may be used together.
.sp
The caller must initialize
.bd
addrbits.
It is copied in with the rest of the structure, and an
uninitialized word asks the driver to swizzle whatever
bits happen to be set.  The callers in this tree zero
the structure, or the field, before filling it in.
.sp
When the driver returns, the whole block is copied back
to the caller, including any bytes the driver wrote into
it.  A bus that answers a command inside the block - the
DJ-DMA leaves a status byte in each command it runs -
has no other way to be heard, so a caller that wants
that answer finds it in
.bd
cmd
after the call.
.sp
.bd
CHARACTER DEVICES
.sp
On a character device
.bd
cmd
is one of the device's own request codes and
.bd
arg
is whatever that request wants.  A terminal has two:
.nf
.bd 2
TIOCGETA    0x7401    fill arg with the terminal's status
TIOCSETA    0x7402    set the terminal's status from arg
.fi
.sp
The status is this six-byte structure:
.nf
.bd 7
struct sgtty {
    UINT8 ispeed;       /* input speed */
    UINT8 ospeed;       /* output speed */
    UINT8 erase;        /* erase character */
    UINT8 kill;         /* kill character */
    UINT16 mode;        /* see <sys/sgtty.h> for the bits */
};
.fi
.sp
They are the requests behind
.bd
gtty
(3) and
.bd
stty
(3); those are the calls to make, and this is the call
under them.  Both were system calls once, and a binary
built before the library moved them here still reaches
the same two requests through the old numbers (gtty =
32, stty = 31).  Any other character device has its own
request codes - the network card's are the NET* requests
of <sys/net.h> - and a code its driver does not have
answers ENOTTY.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
0 if the request ran, or -1 if it did not.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ERRORS
.in +5
.br
.bd
EINVAL
if cmd is not CDBCMD, if len is less than 1 or greater
than CDBMAX, or if count is negative or greater than
CDBDATA.
.br
.bd
EBADF
if fd is not an open file.
.br
.bd
ENOTTY
if fd is not a block or character device, or the device
does not have the request: a block driver with no command
block to run, or a character driver with no such request.
.br
.bd
EIO
if the command failed and the driver gave no other
reason.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ASSEMBLER
.in +5
.br
.bd
(ioctl = 54)
.br
.bd
(fd in hl, cmd and arg on the stack)
.br
.bd
sys; ioctl; cmd; arg
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURNS
.in +5
.br
0 is returned on success.  The carry flag is set on
failure, and the error number is then in hl.
